1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,114
Right at the bottom of the land in Ayodhya, a handful of charcoal was found.

2
00:00:04,114 --> 00:00:07,405
That charcoal gave a date: 910 BCE.

3
00:00:07,405 --> 00:00:11,930
That means even 910 years before Christ, people lived here,

4
00:00:11,930 --> 00:00:15,633
and lit cooking fires. This is not a story,

5
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:17,690
this is a lab report.

6
00:00:17,690 --> 00:00:21,388
This report is from the Archaeological Survey of India, that is, the ASI.

7
00:00:21,388 --> 00:00:23,606
The ASI is a government body of the country

8
00:00:23,606 --> 00:00:26,934
that excavates and looks after old sites.

9
00:00:26,934 --> 00:00:29,892
But that report did not have just charcoal.

10
00:00:29,892 --> 00:00:33,220
On some points, scholars still argue even today.

11
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:36,559
The foundation of a big building was also found under the ground.

12
00:00:36,559 --> 00:00:39,897
Some said this was a huge building with pillars.

13
00:00:39,897 --> 00:00:40,899
Some said,

14
00:00:40,899 --> 00:00:43,904
these are pieces of floors from different periods.

15
00:00:43,904 --> 00:00:45,907
There are educated people on both sides,

16
00:00:45,907 --> 00:00:47,910
and both have arguments.

17
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:51,139
We will go with the evidence, and we begin with the ancient texts.

18
00:00:51,139 --> 00:00:54,369
First, let's see how the old texts show Ayodhya.

19
00:00:54,369 --> 00:00:56,717
Because the meaning of the excavation only makes sense then,

20
00:00:56,717 --> 00:01:00,240
when we know what people were looking for under the ground.

21
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:03,138
The first part of Valmiki's Ramayana is the Balakanda.

22
00:01:03,138 --> 00:01:07,691
In it, Ayodhya is a fortified capital on the banks of the Saryu river.

23
00:01:07,691 --> 00:01:11,831
A safe and beautiful city, surrounded by strong walls.

24
00:01:11,831 --> 00:01:15,970
According to the Ramayana, this city was founded by Manu.

25
00:01:15,970 --> 00:01:17,232
And its size?

26
00:01:17,232 --> 00:01:21,018
According to the Balakanda, Ayodhya was 12 yojana long.

27
00:01:21,018 --> 00:01:24,805
Yojana is an old unit for measuring distance.

28
00:01:24,805 --> 00:01:29,432
Scholars calculate it differently in today's kilometres.

29
00:01:29,432 --> 00:01:34,060
But the message is clear: this is a picture of a huge city.

30
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:37,773
Then comes the Skanda Purana, one part of which is the Ayodhya Mahatmya.

31
00:01:37,773 --> 00:01:41,823
A Mahatmya is a text that describes the glory of a pilgrimage place and gives its layout.

32
00:01:41,823 --> 00:01:45,198
In it, the whole geography of Ayodhya's holy places is written.

33
00:01:45,198 --> 00:01:48,910
Which place is where, which ashram is in which direction.

34
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:52,817
In this same text, the location of the Janmasthan is also given by directions.

35
00:01:52,817 --> 00:01:56,398
It is east of Vighneshwar, and north of Vashishtha.

36
00:01:56,398 --> 00:01:59,328
And also west of the ashram of sage Lomash.

37
00:01:59,328 --> 00:02:02,258
So the text gives a kind of old map,

38
00:02:02,258 --> 00:02:03,560
without any map.

39
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,218
Besides the texts, there are also stories passed on by word of mouth.

40
00:02:07,218 --> 00:02:11,689
One such story is recorded in the old District Gazetteer of Faizabad.

41
00:02:11,689 --> 00:02:13,721
A gazetteer means a government book,

42
00:02:13,721 --> 00:02:17,379
in which the history and present condition of a district is written.

43
00:02:17,379 --> 00:02:21,850
The story says King Vikramaditya resettled Ayodhya.

44
00:02:21,850 --> 00:02:26,014
And he built a magnificent temple, standing on 84 pillars.

45
00:02:26,014 --> 00:02:30,179
The pillars were of black stone, which people called kasauti.

46
00:02:30,179 --> 00:02:32,261
Please note, this is a legend,

47
00:02:32,261 --> 00:02:35,176
not backed by any scripture or excavation.

48
00:02:35,176 --> 00:02:39,340
But such stories have always kept people's curiosity alive.

49
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:42,686
Now the story comes out of the books and goes down into the soil.

50
00:02:42,686 --> 00:02:46,367
The year is 2003, and a big excavation begins in Ayodhya.

51
00:02:46,367 --> 00:02:50,048
Many people were watching this excavation. Let's see

52
00:02:50,048 --> 00:02:52,390
what the ASI did, and how they did it.

53
00:02:52,390 --> 00:02:56,256
From March to August, the ASI team worked here.

54
00:02:56,256 --> 00:02:59,419
This excavation was not being done on their own wish.

55
00:02:59,419 --> 00:03:03,285
The Lucknow bench of the Allahabad High Court had ordered it.

56
00:03:03,285 --> 00:03:06,800
So every step had to be taken carefully, and in writing.

57
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,611
Before the excavation, another investigation had been done,

58
00:03:09,611 --> 00:03:13,475
a Ground Penetrating Radar survey. That means sending radar into the ground,

59
00:03:13,475 --> 00:03:15,935
to take a picture of hidden things without digging.

60
00:03:15,935 --> 00:03:19,799
The radar showed some strange marks in a few places, called anomalies.

61
00:03:19,799 --> 00:03:22,610
The ASI had to check the truth behind these very marks.

62
00:03:22,610 --> 00:03:25,283
For this, the ASI dug 82 trenches.

63
00:03:25,283 --> 00:03:27,573
A trench means a square pit for excavation,

64
00:03:27,573 --> 00:03:30,628
in which soil is removed layer by layer.

65
00:03:30,628 --> 00:03:34,446
Each trench was like a window to peek into the past.

66
00:03:34,446 --> 00:03:37,500
And every window showed something.

67
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:40,998
In an excavation, the layers of soil are the most important evidence.

68
00:03:40,998 --> 00:03:42,047
Layer means stratum,

69
00:03:42,047 --> 00:03:45,545
the level of soil that settled during one period.

70
00:03:45,545 --> 00:03:48,343
The oldest layer is at the bottom, and newer ones on top.

71
00:03:48,343 --> 00:03:52,190
Like newspapers piled one over another, the bottom one is the oldest.

72
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:54,385
The team had archaeologists, laborers,

73
00:03:54,385 --> 00:03:58,409
and supervising officers too. Baskets of soil were sieved,

74
00:03:58,409 --> 00:04:02,067
and the pieces found were kept separately. Every item was photographed,

75
00:04:02,067 --> 00:04:03,896
and its map and depth were recorded.

76
00:04:03,896 --> 00:04:07,920
Because every page of this excavation had to be checked later.

77
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:11,553
Now let's come to the real question: what did the soil tell us?

78
00:04:11,553 --> 00:04:14,460
Let's start with the bottom-most layer.

79
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:18,457
Because that is what tells us when humans first came here.

80
00:04:18,457 --> 00:04:22,090
The story of an excavation is always read from the bottom up.

81
00:04:22,090 --> 00:04:26,058
The ASI named the oldest layer Period I.

82
00:04:26,058 --> 00:04:30,026
In this layer they found special pottery, Northern Black Polished Ware.

83
00:04:30,026 --> 00:04:32,010
That means black, shiny, thin pots,

84
00:04:32,010 --> 00:04:35,978
which are seen as the mark of ancient Indian cities.

85
00:04:35,978 --> 00:04:40,740
According to the ASI, these were the first people to settle at this site.

86
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:43,451
Clay figurines were also found in this same layer,

87
00:04:43,451 --> 00:04:44,807
called terracotta.

88
00:04:44,807 --> 00:04:47,518
That means small figures made of baked clay.

89
00:04:47,518 --> 00:04:49,551
Glass beads were found along with them,

90
00:04:49,551 --> 00:04:51,923
which were probably strung into jewellery.

91
00:04:51,923 --> 00:04:54,973
Everything shows there was a settlement here,

92
00:04:54,973 --> 00:04:55,990
not just a camp.

93
00:04:55,990 --> 00:05:00,274
And then they found a round seal with something written on it.

94
00:05:00,274 --> 00:05:02,221
The writing was in Ashokan Brahmi.

95
00:05:02,221 --> 00:05:07,673
Brahmi is one of the oldest readable scripts of India.

96
00:05:07,673 --> 00:05:11,567
The same script found on Emperor Ashoka's rock inscriptions.

97
00:05:11,567 --> 00:05:14,293
One small seal, but a big question,

98
00:05:14,293 --> 00:05:16,240
who would have used it?

99
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:20,312
Now, about the charcoal we started this video with.

100
00:05:20,312 --> 00:05:24,014
Pieces of charcoal were taken out from the Period I layer.

101
00:05:24,014 --> 00:05:25,865
Then they were radiocarbon dated.

102
00:05:25,865 --> 00:05:28,457
Radiocarbon dating means using science to work out

103
00:05:28,457 --> 00:05:31,048
how old something organic is.

104
00:05:31,048 --> 00:05:34,010
Organic means something that was once alive, like wood.

105
00:05:34,010 --> 00:05:36,238
The result came out around 910 BCE.

106
00:05:36,238 --> 00:05:39,581
Another sample also came out close to the same period.

107
00:05:39,581 --> 00:05:42,923
That means in very ancient times, even before Ashoka,

108
00:05:42,923 --> 00:05:44,409
people lived here.

109
00:05:44,409 --> 00:05:47,380
The report itself admits that in such dates,

110
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:49,980
there is some room for error.

111
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:53,353
Now let's move to the upper layers, the medieval period.

112
00:05:53,353 --> 00:05:56,725
The medieval period means the long era between ancient and modern times.

113
00:05:56,725 --> 00:05:58,749
Here the ASI found something

114
00:05:58,749 --> 00:06:00,772
that got the most attention.

115
00:06:00,772 --> 00:06:03,470
Traces of a large building under the ground.

116
00:06:03,470 --> 00:06:07,644
According to the ASI, this building was about 50 metres long.

117
00:06:07,644 --> 00:06:10,149
Its direction was north to south.

118
00:06:10,149 --> 00:06:13,488
The report called it early medieval. That means,

119
00:06:13,488 --> 00:06:18,080
the foundation of some very big structure that once stood here.

120
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:21,867
The report also records the bases of several pillars linked to this building.

121
00:06:21,867 --> 00:06:25,071
The base of a pillar is the platform on which the pillar stands.

122
00:06:25,071 --> 00:06:26,236
The ASI said,

123
00:06:26,236 --> 00:06:28,857
these bases belonged to a large hall with many pillars.

124
00:06:28,857 --> 00:06:31,770
That means there once stood a huge building resting on pillars.

125
00:06:31,770 --> 00:06:35,620
From here the story stops being straightforward. The ASI report came out,

126
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:37,930
and with it came questions.

127
00:06:37,930 --> 00:06:41,395
Some independent archaeologists read the report carefully.

128
00:06:41,395 --> 00:06:45,245
Independent means people who were not part of this excavation team.

129
00:06:45,245 --> 00:06:47,940
And they put forward a different picture.

130
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:51,099
They said that what has been called pillar bases

131
00:06:51,099 --> 00:06:53,206
are actually something else.

132
00:06:53,206 --> 00:06:56,716
According to them, these were pieces of a brick floor.

133
00:06:56,716 --> 00:06:59,524
And these pieces are not from the same period,

134
00:06:59,524 --> 00:07:03,035
they are from different eras. That is, layers from different times,

135
00:07:03,035 --> 00:07:04,790
were showing up together.

136
00:07:04,790 --> 00:07:06,679
This argument has big implications.

137
00:07:06,679 --> 00:07:08,882
If the bases are from different eras,

138
00:07:08,882 --> 00:07:11,400
then they cannot belong to one hall.

139
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:13,604
Then they are not load-bearing pillars,

140
00:07:13,604 --> 00:07:15,177
just parts of a floor.

141
00:07:15,177 --> 00:07:18,640
In this view, a huge pillared building is not proven.

142
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:21,333
On the other hand, the ASI stood by its report.

143
00:07:21,333 --> 00:07:25,035
According to it, the length and direction of the foundation, and the remaining structure,

144
00:07:25,035 --> 00:07:27,728
point toward a large construction.

145
00:07:27,728 --> 00:07:29,411
Both sides have trained archaeologists.

146
00:07:29,411 --> 00:07:33,450
And both are reading the same soil differently.

147
00:07:33,450 --> 00:07:35,826
This often happens in archaeology.

148
00:07:35,826 --> 00:07:40,181
Archaeology means the science of reading the past through evidence buried in the soil.

149
00:07:40,181 --> 00:07:43,349
The soil does not speak by itself, it has to be read.

150
00:07:43,349 --> 00:07:46,913
A piece of brick is a base for one person,

151
00:07:46,913 --> 00:07:50,476
a floor for another. That is why even after the report,

152
00:07:50,476 --> 00:07:52,060
the debate does not end.

153
00:07:52,060 --> 00:07:55,350
Now the part that is often left out of the news.

154
00:07:55,350 --> 00:07:58,640
The question that even the excavation has no clear answer to.

155
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:01,931
In an honest story, "I don't know" is also an answer.

156
00:08:01,931 --> 00:08:05,550
And following the evidence means saying that plainly too.

157
00:08:05,550 --> 00:08:08,523
Suppose there was a large medieval building there.

158
00:08:08,523 --> 00:08:11,497
Then the question arises, how did it come to an end?

159
00:08:11,497 --> 00:08:14,842
Did it slowly fall into ruins over time?

160
00:08:14,842 --> 00:08:17,443
Or was it deliberately brought down?

161
00:08:17,443 --> 00:08:21,160
The excavation evidence does not clearly settle this question.

162
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:23,816
Between that building and the later construction,

163
00:08:23,816 --> 00:08:27,135
there is a gap of many centuries. Over such a long time, rain,

164
00:08:27,135 --> 00:08:29,791
floods and time all do their work.

165
00:08:29,791 --> 00:08:32,446
The date of something falling into the soil is not written anywhere.

166
00:08:32,446 --> 00:08:34,770
That is why this question is still open today.

167
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:38,691
There are more questions left. How big was the oldest settlement?

168
00:08:38,691 --> 00:08:41,899
Whose seal was it, and what did its owner do?

169
00:08:41,899 --> 00:08:45,463
Where does the Ayodhya of the scriptures meet the Ayodhya of the soil,

170
00:08:45,463 --> 00:08:46,176
and where do they differ?

171
00:08:46,176 --> 00:08:49,740
Only further research can shed light on these.

172
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:53,585
If you visit Ayodhya today, begin at the Saryu ghat.

173
00:08:53,585 --> 00:08:56,031
The same river that is mentioned in the Balakanda.

174
00:08:56,031 --> 00:08:59,526
In the evening aarti, the ghat fills up with lamps.

175
00:08:59,526 --> 00:09:01,973
The river of the scriptures and the river of today

176
00:09:01,973 --> 00:09:04,070
flow under the same name.

177
00:09:04,070 --> 00:09:07,747
Walk through the lanes of the old city, see the temples and ghats.

178
00:09:07,747 --> 00:09:11,791
The place where the excavation was done is a place of worship today.

179
00:09:11,791 --> 00:09:13,630
Devotees come there every day.

180
00:09:13,630 --> 00:09:16,203
And those who want to read the evidence themselves

181
00:09:16,203 --> 00:09:19,880
can start with the ASI website, asi.nic.in.

182
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:24,038
If you see black, shiny NBPW pottery in a museum,

183
00:09:24,038 --> 00:09:26,533
stop. Look at it carefully.

184
00:09:26,533 --> 00:09:31,523
These are the pots that were found in the oldest layer of Ayodhya.

185
00:09:31,523 --> 00:09:34,850
A small piece, and a very long story.

186
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:37,583
So what is the sum of it all? The scriptures show a grand,

187
00:09:37,583 --> 00:09:39,536
fortified Ayodhya.

188
00:09:39,536 --> 00:09:43,441
The soil tells us that people had settled here long ago,

189
00:09:43,441 --> 00:09:45,784
and left behind pots, beads and seals.

190
00:09:45,784 --> 00:09:48,127
About one big building in between,

191
00:09:48,127 --> 00:09:50,860
scholars still hold two opinions today.

192
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:53,233
And the biggest question is this.

193
00:09:53,233 --> 00:09:57,583
Will the scriptures and the soil ever tell one and the same story completely?

194
00:09:57,583 --> 00:10:01,933
Maybe new methods and new research will open up some more layers. Until then,

195
00:10:01,933 --> 00:10:05,888
call what is certain certain, and what is open,

196
00:10:05,888 --> 00:10:07,470
let it stay open.

197
00:10:07,470 --> 00:10:11,018
Next time we will head to the sea, to Dwarka.

198
00:10:11,018 --> 00:10:15,452
They say Krishna's city sank into the water.

199
00:10:15,452 --> 00:10:19,887
What did the divers see there, and what does science say?

200
00:10:19,887 --> 00:10:22,548
But before that, tell us in the comments.

201
00:10:22,548 --> 00:10:27,426
What surprised you the most: the coal from 910 BCE,

202
00:10:27,426 --> 00:10:29,200
or the seal with the Brahmi script?
